{"verdict":"needs-attention","summary":"No-ship for 2a migration as written: v7 fixes the named gaps directionally, but the durable evidence path is internally inconsistent enough to make verified rows fail or lose their audit trail.","findings":[{"severity":"medium","title":"Codex review archive naming is inconsistent with the validator contract","body":"The validator contract requires `_archive/codex-reviews/<session_id>.output` and compares it with `sha256sums.txt`, but the proposed archive command copies each log to `$(basename $log)`. For the second glob, that produces `*.log`, not `<session_id>.output`; for the first glob, the basename may be a task/output filename rather than the `codex-review-<session_id>` value in the manifest. This means a valid `claim_mapping_verified_by = codex-review-<session_id>` can be rejected because the durable file is archived under a different name, or worse, the archived evidence cannot be traced back to the manifest token. This blocks N2 from being reliably enforceable.","file":"plan.md","line_start":704,"line_end":715,"confidence":0.87,"recommendation":"Specify a deterministic extraction rule that maps each `codex-review-<session_id>` token to exactly `_archive/codex-reviews/<session_id>.output`, reject logs whose session_id cannot be parsed, and generate `sha256sums.txt` over those normalized `.output` filenames only."},{"severity":"medium","title":"Optional codex evidence hash storage leaves the integrity check under-specified","body":"The plan says the manifest may add `codex_evidence_sha256` or the validator may compare the archive hash with a hash embedded in free-form `notes`. That is not a durable machine contract: free-form notes are easy to omit, mistype, or edit without schema validation, and the existing 14-column schema does not contain a required hash column. Fixture 16 expects a hash mismatch to fail, but the plan does not define a mandatory place where the expected hash lives, so an implementation can satisfy the prose by merely hashing the archived file against a freshly generated `sha256sums.txt`, which would not detect tampering after archive creation if both file and sums are updated together.","file":"plan.md","line_start":615,"line_end":619,"confidence":0.78,"recommendation":"Add `codex_evidence_sha256` as a required manifest column for every `codex-review-*` verified row, require it to match both the archived file and `sha256sums.txt`, and update the schema/test list from 14 columns accordingly."},{"severity":"medium","title":"Exact inline citation marker can pass without binding to the manifest source_id/raw_path","body":"For self-cited lines, the validator must match `source_id` and `raw_path` values, but one allowed marker format is a generic inline citation like `(<source_id>, <page/section ref>)`. That format has no `raw_path`, so it cannot satisfy the stated equality check against the manifest's `raw_path`. If implemented literally, legitimate inline citations become false negatives; if implemented leniently, claim-mapping can pass without proving the row cites the intended raw artifact. Either outcome undermines N3's claim-mapping guarantee.","file":"plan.md","line_start":635,"line_end":643,"confidence":0.74,"recommendation":"Make every accepted exact marker carry machine-readable `source_id` and `raw_path` fields, or explicitly define inline citations as valid only after resolving `source_id` through `textbook/sources.yml` to the manifest `raw_path`."}],"next_steps":["Do not start 2a migration until the codex-review evidence filename/hash contract is made deterministic.","Tighten the self-cited-line marker grammar so all accepted forms can be validated against manifest fields."]}
